import {defineLesson as L} from './lesson-schema.js';
export const foundations=[
L({id:'linux-start',chapter:'linux',title:'What Linux actually is',description:'Start with the machine, the kernel, the shell and the programs you interact with.',scene:'overview',source:'Embedded-Linux-Essentials-Handbook.pdf',sourceChapter:'Embedded Linux architecture and root filesystem',goals:['Distinguish Linux from a distribution','Separate kernel space from userspace','Identify the four parts of an embedded Linux system'],steps:[
['Computer','Begin with a computer, not a command','A computer combines a processor, memory, storage and devices. The processor executes instructions; RAM holds working state; persistent storage retains files when power disappears. On an embedded board, many controllers share one system-on-chip. An operating system coordinates these resources so each application does not need to initialize the entire machine.','Keep RAM and persistent storage separate in your mental model: a reboot loses one but normally preserves the other.','uname -m','Reports the architecture of the running system; for example aarch64 or x86_64.'],
['Kernel','The kernel manages access to the machine','Linux is the kernel: the privileged program managing processes, virtual memory, filesystems, networking and drivers. Applications normally request services through system calls instead of writing directly to hardware. This boundary provides isolation and a stable interface. A shell command is usually a userspace executable, not a command built into the kernel.','A driver belongs to the hardware-facing side of the system; an application uses the interface the kernel exposes.','uname -r','Kernel release string of the system on which you run the command.'],
['Userspace','Userspace makes the system useful','A usable Linux system also needs libraries, an initial process, utilities and applications. A distribution assembles these pieces into a coherent installation. Desktop distributions make broad choices for general machines. An embedded image selects the pieces needed for one product, often with a much smaller filesystem and a deliberately limited service set.','Linux alone is not a complete product image. You need a kernel and a matching userspace.','ps -p 1 -o comm=','Shows the first userspace process, often systemd, init or another supervisor.'],
['Shell','The shell is an interpreter','A terminal displays text and accepts input. A shell reads that input and interprets commands, variables, redirections and pipelines. It starts programs and reports their exit status. Bash is common on development hosts; BusyBox ash is common on small targets. Features of one shell are not automatically portable to another.','Distinguish the terminal window from the shell process running inside it.','printf "hello Linux\\n"\nprintf "%s\\n" "$0"','A greeting followed by the name used to invoke the current shell.'],
['Embedded','Four artifacts make a bootable product','A representative embedded Linux product combines a toolchain, bootloader, kernel and root filesystem. The toolchain produces target programs on a development machine. The bootloader loads the kernel and boot inputs. The kernel discovers and controls hardware. The root filesystem supplies the libraries, init process and applications. Later chapters build and connect these parts.','A failure at one boundary is easier to diagnose when you can name the artifact involved.','cat /proc/version\nls /','Kernel build identification, followed by the top-level filesystem directories.'],
['Workspace','Use a safe development environment','Start in a Linux VM, supported Linux workstation or a compatible WSL2 development environment. Keep a dedicated lab directory and avoid practising privileged commands on valuable data. QEMU lets you learn target behavior without a physical board. Real hardware becomes necessary when studying electrical interfaces, board-specific boot or actual timing behavior.','Use emulation for repeatable software experiments; use a board to validate hardware assumptions.','mkdir -p ~/embedded-labs\ncd ~/embedded-labs\npwd','A dedicated working directory under your home directory.']
],lab:{objective:'Identify the running kernel, architecture, PID 1 and root filesystem on a Linux environment.',setup:'A Linux terminal; these inspection commands do not modify the operating system.',commands:'uname -srmo\ncat /proc/version\nps -p 1 -o pid,comm,args\nfindmnt /\nls /',expected:'Record which values describe the kernel, which describe userspace, and where persistent root storage is mounted.',troubleshooting:'Containers and WSL can have a different init process and mount layout from a physical board. Describe what you observe rather than forcing an expected name.',deliverable:'A one-page map of your host: CPU architecture, kernel, init process and root mount.'},quiz:{question:'Which component directly manages processes, memory and device drivers?',options:['The terminal window','The Linux kernel','The package repository'],answer:1,why:'The kernel provides privileged resource management; the terminal and package tools are userspace.'}}),
L({id:'shell-files',chapter:'linux',title:'Navigate files & directories',description:'Understand paths, directory trees, links and the difference between a name and a file.',scene:'filesystem',goals:['Resolve absolute and relative paths','Read directory listings','Explain symbolic links and mount points'],steps:[
['Paths','A path is a route through a directory tree','Linux presents one tree beginning at /. An absolute path starts there; a relative path starts in the current working directory. A home directory is normally under /home, while ~ is a shell expansion for a home location. The names . and .. refer to the current directory and its parent. File names are case-sensitive on typical Linux filesystems.','Always establish your working directory before interpreting a relative path.','pwd\nls -la\ncd ..','Your current path, directory entries including hidden names, and a move to the parent.'],
['Layout','Learn the roles of the main directories','/etc stores system configuration; /usr commonly holds installed programs and shared data; /var holds variable state such as logs; /tmp is temporary space. /proc and /sys expose kernel and device state through virtual filesystems. /dev exposes device interfaces. Some distributions merge /bin and /sbin into /usr using links.','A file-like interface under /proc or /sys may report live state rather than occupy a regular disk file.','ls -ld /bin /usr/bin /proc /sys /dev','Compare directory and symbolic-link entries on your system.'],
['Inspect','Inspect before changing','ls lists names and metadata, file identifies content type, and stat shows detailed metadata. cat prints a small text file; less supports navigation through larger output. Do not assume an unfamiliar file is text, and do not dump arbitrary device nodes. Inspection tools let you identify the artifact before choosing how to edit or execute it.','A filename extension is a convention, not the authority on file contents.','file /bin/sh\nstat /etc/passwd\nhead -n 5 /etc/passwd','Program type, ownership/timestamps, and a short view of account database entries.'],
['Create','Practise changes inside the lab directory','mkdir creates directories; cp copies; mv renames or moves. Quoting keeps a path containing spaces as one argument. Shell wildcards expand before the program runs, so inspect what they match. Build the habit of operating within a known workspace. Deleting files with rm generally bypasses a graphical trash mechanism.','Quote path arguments and inspect broad matches before using them in modifying commands.','mkdir -p ~/embedded-labs/files\ncd ~/embedded-labs/files\nprintf "sample\\n" > notes.txt\ncp notes.txt notes-copy.txt','Two independent text files containing the same initial content.'],
['Links','Names can point to the same data','A symbolic link stores another path and can dangle if its destination disappears. A hard link is another directory entry referring to the same inode on the same filesystem. Removing one hard-link name does not remove data while another link remains. Embedded root filesystems often use symlinks for multi-call utilities and library versioning.','A copied file, a symbolic link and a hard link have different update behavior.','ln -s notes.txt notes-link\nls -l notes-link\nreadlink notes-link','The link points to notes.txt; changing the original is visible through the link.'],
['Mounts','Mounting joins filesystems into the tree','A mount point is a directory where another filesystem becomes visible. The path /data might live on a separate partition while still appearing in the same tree. Files originally underneath a mount point are hidden while another filesystem is mounted there. df reports filesystem capacity; du measures reachable file usage. These tools answer different questions.','A pathname alone does not reveal which storage device backs it.','findmnt\ndf -h\ndu -sh ~/embedded-labs','Mount relationships, filesystem capacity and the lab directory size.']
],lab:{objective:'Build a small directory tree and predict how copies and links react to a source change.',setup:'Use ~/embedded-labs/files only.',commands:'mkdir -p ~/embedded-labs/files\ncd ~/embedded-labs/files\nprintf "version one\\n" > original.txt\ncp original.txt copy.txt\nln -s original.txt symbolic.txt\nln original.txt hard.txt\nprintf "version two\\n" > original.txt\nls -li\ncat original.txt copy.txt symbolic.txt hard.txt',expected:'The copy keeps the old contents. The symbolic link and hard link expose the changed original. The hard-link inode number matches the original.',troubleshooting:'If a link already exists, use a fresh lab subdirectory rather than overwriting unknown files. Hard links cannot cross filesystems.',deliverable:'A table of names, inode numbers, link targets and observed contents.'},quiz:{question:'Which path is absolute?',options:['../etc/config','/etc/config','config/settings'],answer:1,why:'An absolute path starts with / and resolves from the root of the filesystem tree.'}}),
L({id:'shell-pipes',chapter:'linux',title:'Commands, pipes & shell scripts',description:'Compose small tools, preserve arguments, inspect errors and automate repeatable work.',scene:'pipeline',goals:['Explain standard input/output/error','Use quoting and pipelines','Write a checked shell script'],steps:[
['Arguments','The shell constructs an argument list','A command line becomes a program name and arguments after shell expansion. Unquoted whitespace separates arguments; double quotes preserve spaces while allowing variable expansion; single quotes preserve literal text. A variable should usually be quoted when passed as an argument. Understanding this prevents many accidental path and recipe-script bugs.','Quote values whose contents should stay one argument.','name="sensor gateway"\nprintf "<%s>\\n" "$name"','One argument containing a space, printed between angle brackets.'],
['Streams','Programs communicate through three standard streams','Standard input is descriptor 0, standard output is 1, and standard error is 2. Redirection can write output to a file or read input from one. A pipeline connects one program’s stdout to the next program’s stdin; stderr does not automatically join the pipe. This separation makes tools composable and errors visible.','A pipeline is a data path, not an instruction to merge every kind of output.','printf "one\\ntwo\\n" | wc -l','The line count is 2.'],
['Filter','Combine tools to answer a narrow question','grep selects matching lines, sort orders them, and uniq can count adjacent duplicates. head and tail bound output. Avoid building a complicated parser for structured data when a structured tool exists, but these text tools are effective for controlled logs. Decide whether you need a fixed string or a regular expression.','First inspect sample input; then design the filtering step.','printf "sensor\\nnetwork\\nsensor\\n" | sort | uniq -c','A count for network and a count of two for sensor.'],
['Status','Exit status is the machine-readable result','A successful command normally returns zero. $? refers to the preceding command status, so another command can overwrite it. An if statement can test a command directly. Pipeline status behavior varies by shell; Bash pipefail can expose earlier failures, but it is not a portable POSIX shell option.','Output that looks successful is not a substitute for checking the exit status.','if grep -q sensor /etc/hostname; then\n  printf "match\\n"\nelse\n  printf "no match or read error\\n"\nfi','The branch depends on the file content and grep status.'],
['Script','Give automation an explicit contract','A script should document its inputs, validate required paths and produce meaningful status codes. Use a shebang for the intended interpreter. Prefer simple control flow over relying on subtle set -e behavior. Never assume every embedded shell supports Bash arrays or process substitution. Choose portable syntax when the target only has /bin/sh.','A script that detects failure and stops is easier to trust than one that continues with partial results.','#!/bin/sh\n[ -r "$1" ] || { printf "input unreadable\\n" >&2; exit 1; }\nwc -l < "$1"','A small script contract: one readable input path, one line count on success.'],
['Environment','Environment variables cross the process boundary','A shell variable belongs to that shell unless exported. A child receives an environment copy; changes in a child do not update its parent. Sourcing a file executes it in the current shell, which is why SDK environment scripts are sourced. Only source trusted scripts: they can change your environment and execute commands.','Executing an SDK setup script is different from sourcing it.','export SENSOR_MODE=demo\nsh -c \'printf "%s\\n" "$SENSOR_MODE"\'','The child shell receives SENSOR_MODE=demo.']
],lab:{objective:'Write a script that summarizes a controlled log and reports an unreadable input correctly.',setup:'Create a small log in a dedicated lab directory. Use a text editor for the script.',commands:'printf "INFO start\\nERROR sensor\\nINFO ready\\nERROR timeout\\n" > sample.log\ngrep -c ERROR sample.log\nsh -n summarize.sh\nsh summarize.sh sample.log\nsh summarize.sh missing.log',expected:'Two ERROR lines for the sample. Missing input produces a message on stderr and a nonzero status.',troubleshooting:'Check quoting with paths containing spaces. sh -n checks syntax, not every runtime error.',deliverable:'The script, sample input and recorded success/failure exit codes.'},quiz:{question:'What does a pipe normally connect?',options:['The first command’s stderr to disk','The first command’s stdout to the next command’s stdin','Two processes’ complete memory spaces'],answer:1,why:'Pipelines connect standard streams; stderr requires separate redirection.'}}),
L({id:'permissions',chapter:'linux',title:'Users, permissions & least privilege',description:'Read ownership and mode bits before reaching for sudo or chmod 777.',scene:'security',goals:['Interpret rwx bits on files and directories','Understand user and group ownership','Design a restricted service account'],steps:[
['Identity','Processes act with an identity','Linux permission checks depend on process credentials such as user and group IDs. File ownership is stored numerically; account databases translate IDs to names. The same numeric owner can appear under a different name on another system. Embedded image generation must deliberately create the accounts and ownership required by the runtime.','Copying names is not the same as preserving identity semantics.','id\nls -ln /etc/passwd','Your current IDs and numeric ownership of a system file.'],
['Bits','Read the three permission triplets','For regular files, read permits reading content, write permits modifying content, and execute permits execution. The three triplets apply to owner, group and other users. Mode 0644 commonly means an owner-writable file readable by everyone; 0755 commonly means an executable readable and executable by others. These are conventions, not universal policies.','Changing file content permissions and changing ownership solve different problems.','ls -l /bin/sh\nstat -c "%a %U %G" /etc/passwd','Symbolic and numeric views of permissions.'],
['Directories','Directory permissions control name lookup','Read on a directory permits listing entries, execute permits traversing/searching names, and write controls modifying entries subject to other rules. Deleting a file is primarily an operation on its parent directory, not merely on the file’s write bit. Directory ownership and the sticky bit therefore matter for shared temporary locations.','An unreadable file can still be removable by someone who can modify its parent directory.','ls -ld /tmp\nnamei -l /etc/passwd','The path’s directory permissions explain whether traversal is possible.'],
['Defaults','umask removes permissions from defaults','A process umask masks bits from creation modes requested by applications. It is not a rule that simply sets all new files to one fixed mode. An application can request more restrictive permissions. For secrets, create files with deliberately narrow access instead of writing broadly accessible content and fixing permissions later.','Check both the application creation mode and the inherited umask.','umask\nmkdir -p ~/embedded-labs/access\nls -ld ~/embedded-labs/access','Compare the resulting directory mode with the current umask.'],
['Services','Give each service only its required access','A sensor daemon may need one device interface and one data directory, not root access to the entire system. Dedicated users, group ownership, udev rules and selected systemd restrictions can narrow access. Capabilities subdivide some privileged operations, but granting a capability still increases authority and needs a specific reason.','Fix the required device or directory access instead of granting unrestricted privileges.','systemctl show sensor.service -p User -p Group','For an installed service, inspect the identity it runs under.'],
['Diagnose','Follow EACCES through the whole path','When access fails, check the process identity, directory traversal, final object mode, filesystem mount options and any mandatory access controls in use. A read-only mount is not repaired by chmod. A service sandbox can also hide paths or restrict writes. Collect the actual error and context before changing policy.','Permission denied is a starting observation, not a complete root cause.','id\nnamei -l /var/lib/sensor\nfindmnt -T /var/lib/sensor','Identity, path permissions and backing mount provide three distinct pieces of evidence.']
],lab:{objective:'Observe file and directory access using a private lab directory without changing system accounts.',setup:'Use your current non-root user. Inspect, rather than modify, any real service.',commands:'mkdir -p ~/embedded-labs/access\ncd ~/embedded-labs/access\nprintf "private\\n" > secret.txt\nchmod 600 secret.txt\nstat -c "%a %U %G" secret.txt\nnamei -l "$PWD/secret.txt"',expected:'The file is owner-readable and owner-writable. Parent traversal permissions remain relevant.',troubleshooting:'Network filesystems, ACLs and security modules may add policy beyond the basic rwx bits.',deliverable:'An explanation of a safe permission layout for a sensor service and its data directory.'},quiz:{question:'A filesystem is mounted read-only. Will chmod make writes to it possible?',options:['Yes, if mode is 777','No; mount policy is separate from file mode','Only if the file has no extension'],answer:1,why:'File permissions cannot override a read-only filesystem mount.'}}),
L({id:'processes',chapter:'linux',title:'Processes, signals & services',description:'Follow a program from exec to exit, and distinguish process lifetime from service health.',scene:'process',goals:['Inspect process state and parentage','Explain signals and graceful shutdown','Separate a process from its supervisor'],steps:[
['Launch','A program file becomes a running process','A program is code stored in a file; a process is an execution context with an address space, credentials, file descriptors and scheduling state. The shell starts an external program as a child. On Unix-like systems, process creation and executable loading are distinct operations; fork and exec provide the familiar model.','Two executions of the same program are separate processes with separate state.','ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm | head','Process IDs, parent IDs, state and command names.'],
['State','Running is only one of several states','A process can be running, runnable, sleeping while waiting for an event, stopped or exited. High latency does not necessarily mean high CPU use: a process can spend time waiting on storage, a lock or another service. ps state codes summarize observations, while tracing can explain the transitions.','Distinguish CPU work from waiting before deciding how to optimize.','ps -eo pid,stat,pcpu,comm | head','Compare process state with sampled CPU usage.'],
['Descriptors','Open resources are part of process state','A file descriptor is a small integer referencing an open file description or another kernel interface such as a socket or pipe. Descriptor leaks can eventually prevent new operations. Parent and child relationships can preserve inherited descriptors unless they are closed or marked close-on-exec. Services should deliberately manage the resources they inherit.','A file can remain in use after its pathname disappears because an open reference still exists.','ls -l /proc/$$/fd','The shell’s open descriptors, including standard input/output/error.'],
['Signals','Request shutdown before forcing termination','SIGTERM requests termination and can be handled so a program flushes state and releases resources. SIGKILL cannot be caught and prevents application cleanup. Neither signal makes storage transactions magically durable. Programs should define shutdown behavior, timeouts and restart recovery. Use a supervisor rather than inventing uncontrolled restart loops.','Graceful termination and crash recovery are complementary requirements.','sleep 60 &\npid=$!\nkill -TERM "$pid"\nwait "$pid"','The process terminates; the shell reports the resulting status.'],
['Reap','The parent must collect child exit status','A terminated child can remain as a zombie until its parent reaps its exit status. It no longer executes application code, but retains process bookkeeping. Orphan handling and reaping matter to supervisors and PID 1. A rapidly restarting service can hide the original fault unless logs and restart rate are inspected.','A restart policy should not substitute for understanding why the program exits.','ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm | grep Z','Any Z entries are zombies; no output is a normal result.'],
['Health','A live process may still be a failed product','A daemon can run while failing to read sensors, connect to a dependency or make forward progress. A service manager monitors process lifetime; readiness notifications and watchdog integration can express more useful health signals. Design health checks around observable functionality with bounded failure detection.','Define what healthy means for the product, not just whether a PID exists.','systemctl status sensor.service','Inspect active state, recent exits and logs on a systemd target.']
],lab:{objective:'Launch a harmless process, inspect its parent and descriptors, then terminate it gracefully.',setup:'Use a Linux terminal. Keep the PID of the sleep process you create.',commands:'sleep 120 &\npid=$!\nps -p "$pid" -o pid,ppid,stat,comm\nls -l "/proc/$pid/fd"\nkill -TERM "$pid"\nwait "$pid"\nprintf "status=%s\\n" "$?"',expected:'The child has a distinct PID, belongs to your shell, and exits after SIGTERM. Its /proc entry disappears after reaping.',troubleshooting:'Inspect your own PID carefully; do not send signals to unrelated processes.',deliverable:'A process-lifecycle sketch and a proposed health check for a gateway daemon.'},quiz:{question:'What does an active process prove about the product?',options:['Every dependency is healthy','Only that the process is alive; functional health needs more checks','That no deadlock is possible'],answer:1,why:'Lifetime, readiness and ongoing functional health are separate properties.'}}),
L({id:'network-basics',chapter:'linux',title:'Networking from the device outward',description:'Build a layered diagnosis from link state through addressing, routing, DNS and sockets.',scene:'network',goals:['Separate link, IP and DNS failures','Read addresses and routes','Use a targeted connectivity check'],steps:[
['Link','Begin with the network interface','A physical or virtual interface must exist and be operational before higher-layer networking can work. Ethernet carrier differs from having an IP address; Wi-Fi also needs association and authentication. If the driver never creates an interface, changing DNS settings cannot repair it. Inspect the lowest failed layer first.','A network symptom should be located at a layer before configuration changes begin.','ip link show','Interface names, administrative state and link indicators.'],
['Address','Addresses identify an interface on a network','An IPv4 address and prefix define local addressing; IPv6 may provide multiple addresses per interface. DHCP can supply addresses and other configuration, while static provisioning must avoid conflicts. An address existing in configuration is not proof that it was applied to the active interface.','Inspect the running configuration, not just a file that might not be used.','ip addr show','Assigned addresses and prefixes for each interface.'],
['Route','The route table chooses where traffic goes','A destination matching the local subnet may be reached directly. Other destinations generally require a gateway route. The selected source address and interface depend on routing and policy. If a board has Ethernet, Wi-Fi and a modem, multiple default routes can produce surprising behavior.','Ask which route the kernel would use for the specific destination.','ip route show\nip route get 192.0.2.1','The second command shows a route decision for a documentation address; it sends no packet.'],
['DNS','Names add a separate dependency','DNS resolution maps names to addresses using the configured resolver path. A numeric-address connection can work while a hostname fails. Conversely, resolving a name proves little about whether the application port is reachable. On systemd-resolved targets, resolvectl can show per-link resolver configuration; other systems use different tools.','Keep resolution, routing and application connectivity as separate checks.','getent hosts localhost\ncat /etc/resolv.conf','Name resolution through the system database and the resolver configuration file.'],
['Transport','A socket connects processes, not just machines','TCP provides a reliable ordered byte stream; UDP provides datagrams without those guarantees. A service must listen on the right address and port. Firewalls, process binding, TLS and application protocol errors are different causes of failure. ICMP ping may be blocked even when a TCP service works.','Test the actual protocol and endpoint the application needs.','ss -lnt\nss -lnu','Listening TCP and UDP sockets, subject to installed tooling.'],
['Resilience','A product must tolerate lost connectivity','A field device should define what happens without a network: local buffering, bounded retry with backoff, stable identity and recovery after reconnect. A fixed retry loop can overload the device or backend. Boot readiness should not accidentally depend on a permanently unavailable internet service.','Network availability is an input to the design, not a guarantee.','journalctl -b -u systemd-networkd','On a networkd-managed target, correlate address and link events with application failures.']
],lab:{objective:'Produce a layered report of the host network without changing network settings.',setup:'A Linux environment with iproute2. Use only endpoints you own for connection tests.',commands:'ip link show\nip addr show\nip route show\nip route get 192.0.2.1\ngetent hosts localhost\nss -lnt',expected:'Identify interfaces, addresses, default routes, local resolver behavior and listening services.',troubleshooting:'Minimal BusyBox images may have fewer options. A container sees its own network namespace rather than the whole host.',deliverable:'A diagnostic decision tree: missing interface, missing address, wrong route, DNS failure, refused connection.'},quiz:{question:'A numeric IP connection works but the equivalent hostname fails. Which layer deserves attention?',options:['DNS/name resolution','DRAM timing','Filesystem inode allocation'],answer:0,why:'Successful numeric connectivity narrows the additional failing dependency to name resolution or name-specific application behavior.'}}),
L({id:'c-build',chapter:'development',title:'C programs: source to executable',description:'Understand preprocessing, compilation, assembly and linking before cross-compiling.',scene:'compile',goals:['Explain each build stage','Distinguish compile and link errors','Inspect an executable'],steps:[
['Source','A source file is not yet machine code','C source expresses declarations and operations. Headers provide declarations shared across translation units. The preprocessor expands includes and macros before the compiler analyzes the resulting program. A missing header is a different failure from a missing library, even when both concern the same component.','Locate the failing build stage before adding flags.','cc --version','Identify the host compiler before running the lab.'],
['Compile','Each translation unit becomes an object','The compiler checks types and emits target instructions, commonly through an assembly stage. Object files contain code, data, symbols and relocation information. Compiling with -c stops before final linking, which is useful for showing that successful compilation does not prove every referenced function has an implementation available.','Headers describe interfaces; object code supplies implementations.','cc -Wall -Wextra -g -c hello.c -o hello.o','An object file is generated; unresolved external references can still exist.'],
['Link','The linker resolves symbols','The linker combines objects and libraries into an executable or shared object. Undefined reference usually indicates a missing implementation, missing library or a link-order/configuration issue. Duplicate definitions can indicate code placed incorrectly in headers or multiple objects defining one global symbol. Read the first useful diagnostic instead of randomly adding libraries.','Compilation success and link success are independent checkpoints.','cc hello.o -o hello\nfile hello','The host executable’s architecture and format.'],
['Load','The dynamic loader supplies runtime libraries','A dynamically linked executable records an interpreter and needed shared objects. Those dependencies must exist in the target image with a compatible ABI. A development header is not needed merely to execute the program, while a runtime shared library may be essential. Static linking changes the dependency model and has its own trade-offs.','A binary can link on the host and still lack required runtime support on the target.','readelf -l hello\nreadelf -d hello','Inspect interpreter and dynamic dependency metadata.'],
['Symbols','Keep debug evidence outside the minimal image','Debug information maps machine addresses back to source lines and variables. Production images often separate symbols from runtime binaries. Retain the exact matching symbols and source revision for field diagnosis. A different build’s symbols can make a backtrace misleading even when filenames look correct.','Small target images and strong debug capability can coexist with archived symbol packages.','readelf -S hello | grep debug','A -g build normally contains debug sections before stripping.']
],lab:{objective:'Build and inspect a minimal C program using separate compile and link stages.',setup:'Create hello.c with stdio.h and a main function calling puts("hello target"). Use the host compiler first.',commands:'cc -Wall -Wextra -g -c hello.c -o hello.o\ncc hello.o -o hello\n./hello\nfile hello.o hello\nreadelf -h hello\nreadelf -d hello',expected:'Explain which artifact is relocatable and which is executable, and identify the architecture.',troubleshooting:'If cc is unavailable, install the development tools appropriate to your Linux distribution. Do not replace system compilers.',deliverable:'Source, object, executable and a short explanation of compilation versus linking.'},quiz:{question:'An undefined reference generally appears during which stage?',options:['Directory creation','Linking','Shell history expansion'],answer:1,why:'The linker resolves symbol references among object files and libraries.'}}),
L({id:'git-workflow',chapter:'development',title:'Git, patches & reproducible work',description:'Track source changes as reviewable commits instead of edits hidden in build directories.',scene:'git',goals:['Separate working tree, staging and commits','Create a reviewable patch','Record exact revisions'],steps:[
['Repository','A commit identifies a complete source state','Git records content and history, with commits identifying trees and parent relationships. A branch is a movable name for a commit, not an immutable release input. For reproducible builds, record commit IDs and configuration instead of assuming a branch will always point to the same source.','A tag or branch name is convenient; a recorded commit ID is the stronger build input.','git status --short\ngit rev-parse HEAD','Working tree changes and the exact current commit.'],
['Diff','Inspect the change before staging it','The working tree contains edits, while the index selects content for the next commit. git diff and git diff --cached answer different questions. Keep generated build output out of the source history unless it is an intentional deliverable. Do not discard existing changes while investigating a build problem.','Review the staged patch as the artifact another engineer will read.','git diff\ngit diff --cached','Unstaged changes followed by staged changes.'],
['Commit','Write a commit around one engineering reason','A useful commit explains what behavior changes and why. Combine related source and test changes, but keep unrelated formatting and generated churn separate. Small coherent commits make review, bisecting and backporting easier. A commit that builds but silently depends on an untracked file is not a reproducible development state.','Reviewability helps future debugging as much as it helps current collaboration.','git add hello.c\ngit diff --cached\ngit commit -m "Add minimal hello application"','The staged change becomes a recorded commit in your lab repository.'],
['Patch','A Yocto patch carries maintenance context','git format-patch produces a patch with author and commit information. Yocto integration commonly adds an Upstream-Status header indicating whether a change is submitted, a backport or intentionally downstream. Signed-off-by has a project-defined contribution meaning; do not invent another person’s sign-off. Track which source revision the patch applies to.','A patch is a maintenance obligation that must survive upstream updates.','git format-patch -1 HEAD','One patch file for the selected commit, created in your lab directory.'],
['Debug','Use history to narrow regressions','git log and git show connect code to its recorded reason. git bisect can locate a first bad commit when you have a reliable pass/fail test and a known-good boundary. The test must isolate the behavior being investigated. Mixed build caches or changing external inputs can otherwise give inconsistent results.','A reproducible test makes history an active debugging tool.','git log --oneline -5\ngit show --stat HEAD','Inspect recent history and the scope of the current change.']
],lab:{objective:'Create a local repository with two coherent commits and inspect the generated patch.',setup:'Use a new lab directory, not an existing project. Configure your own author identity if Git requests it.',commands:'mkdir -p ~/embedded-labs/git-demo\ncd ~/embedded-labs/git-demo\ngit init\nprintf "hello\\n" > notes.txt\ngit add notes.txt\ngit commit -m "Record initial lab notes"\ngit log --oneline\ngit format-patch -1 HEAD',expected:'The commit ID identifies the recorded content, and the patch includes the change and its message.',troubleshooting:'If commit identity is missing, configure the identity you actually use; do not copy a fabricated one from an example.',deliverable:'A patch and a manifest recording the commit ID used for the lab.'},quiz:{question:'Why is a branch name insufficient as an immutable release input?',options:['It cannot contain source code','It can move to another commit','It disables the compiler'],answer:1,why:'Branches are movable references. Record exact source revisions for reproducibility.'}}),
L({id:'build-tools',chapter:'development',title:'Make, CMake & dependency tracking',description:'Make builds incremental without confusing build-system logic with product configuration.',scene:'compile',goals:['Explain dependency-based rebuilds','Use an out-of-tree build','Keep target configuration explicit'],steps:[
['Graph','A build is a graph of outputs and prerequisites','A build tool decides which actions are required by comparing declared dependencies and outputs. Missing dependency declarations can leave stale artifacts; overly broad declarations waste work. Parallel execution is safe only where ordering constraints are represented. A build script that works with -j1 but races with -j8 often has an incomplete graph.','Build correctness depends on declared relationships, not on a lucky execution order.','make -n','Preview commands for an existing Makefile without executing them.'],
['Make','Make recipes execute shell commands','A Make target describes an output and its prerequisites; the recipe describes how to produce it. Automatic variables reduce repeated paths, but shell and Make variable expansion occur at different layers. Distinguish compiler flags from link flags. Avoid hand-maintaining header dependencies when your compiler/build system can generate them.','Changing an input should invalidate every output that depends on it.','make -j2','Build independent targets in parallel when the Makefile declares dependencies correctly.'],
['CMake','CMake generates a native build graph','CMake configures a project and generates build files, which are then consumed by a tool such as Ninja or Make. An out-of-tree build keeps generated artifacts away from source. A toolchain file conveys cross-compilation settings; manually replacing one compiler command after configuration can leave cached host assumptions behind.','Configure for the intended toolchain before building, not halfway through the build.','cmake -S . -B build\ncmake --build build','Separate configuration from compilation.'],
['Install','Installation is a separate operation','A build output is not automatically in the target filesystem layout. Installation rules define destinations, permissions and runtime data. Under Yocto, classes provide the environment and call the build system’s install operation into staging. Hardcoded host paths in install rules bypass that integration and can cause packaging problems.','Make the component installable before trying to wrap it in a recipe.','cmake --install build --prefix "$PWD/staging"','Install the lab application into a local staging prefix.'],
['Diagnose','Observe the actual command line','Verbose output reveals which compiler, include paths and libraries were selected. Cache changes, environment variables and pkg-config can all affect selection. Keep a known-good configuration and inspect differences when moving from a host build to an SDK or BitBake build. Do not solve a target failure by silently linking host libraries.','The command line is evidence of the effective configuration.','cmake --build build --verbose','Compiler and linker invocations for the current build.']
],lab:{objective:'Build the same tiny application out of tree, then install it under a local prefix.',setup:'A minimal CMake project declaring one executable and an install(TARGETS ...) rule.',commands:'cmake -S . -B build\ncmake --build build --verbose\ncmake --install build --prefix "$PWD/staging"\nfind staging -type f',expected:'Generated files remain under build, while the staged executable appears under staging/bin.',troubleshooting:'A missing install rule produces no expected artifact even if compilation succeeded. Inspect configuration output for compiler detection.',deliverable:'An out-of-tree build and a locally staged installation layout.'},quiz:{question:'Why use an out-of-tree build?',options:['To separate generated artifacts from source','To avoid all dependencies','To make host binaries run on every target'],answer:0,why:'It keeps configuration/build outputs separate and makes multiple configurations easier to manage.'}}),
L({id:'build-system-choice',chapter:'yocto',title:'Buildroot, Yocto & the product decision',description:'Understand why you would build a distribution and what complexity each approach buys.',scene:'overview',goals:['Compare build-system boundaries','Separate Yocto, Poky, OE-Core and BitBake','Choose based on a product lifecycle'],steps:[
['Need','A product needs a controlled software supply chain','A desktop package installation is useful for development but does not by itself define a repeatable embedded product. A product build must identify source inputs, select configuration, compile for the target, construct images and preserve evidence. The engineering cost includes updates, legal obligations, vulnerability response and hardware changes over the supported lifetime.','Choose a build process for the lifecycle, not only the first successful boot.','Write product constraints','Target boards, update policy, support duration, image features and release evidence.'],
['Buildroot','Buildroot assembles a coherent system image','Buildroot configures and builds a cross toolchain, kernel, bootloader and userspace using a relatively direct configuration model. It is often attractive for a focused appliance. Understand its workflow for changing configuration and rebuilding; do not assume a package-management distribution model simply because it builds many upstream components.','A smaller metadata model can be valuable when it fits the product requirements.','Inspect a Buildroot defconfig','The saved configuration expresses the selected platform and package options.'],
['Yocto','Yocto provides tools and practices for custom distributions','The Yocto Project is an ecosystem for constructing custom Linux systems. BitBake executes tasks from metadata; OpenEmbedded-Core supplies core recipes and classes; Poky is a reference integration. Layers separate reusable software, BSP support and product policy. These distinctions matter when choosing repositories, reviewing dependencies and deciding where a change belongs.','Yocto is not simply an operating-system image you install.','Inspect the source checkout','Identify BitBake, meta, BSP layers and product layers.'],
['Trade-offs','Flexibility has a maintenance cost','Yocto’s metadata, package splitting, SDK generation and layer model support complex product families, but require disciplined configuration and build infrastructure. Buildroot can reduce initial complexity for some systems. Vendor BSP quality and supported hardware are often decisive. Compare maintenance workflow, team experience, CI capacity and expected product variants.','The right choice is the one whose ownership cost matches the actual product.','Compare two candidate approaches','Record support, customization, rebuild, license and update requirements.'],
['Baseline','Pin a coherent release set','Use compatible branches and revisions for all layers. A new feature described in a newer book is not necessarily present in Scarthgap. Mixing arbitrary layer releases can break class interfaces and metadata assumptions. This course labels a Scarthgap baseline and treats newer mechanisms as release-specific concepts requiring verification before use.','A version-consistent baseline is more valuable than a random collection of newest repositories.','git rev-parse HEAD\nbitbake-layers show-layers','Record source revisions and active layers for the chosen build.']
],lab:{objective:'Write a build-system decision record for a single-board gateway with two hardware variants.',setup:'No build is required. Use product requirements rather than popularity as evidence.',commands:'Decision record sections:\n1. Hardware and BSP availability\n2. Image variants and packages\n3. SDK and application workflow\n4. Update and recovery policy\n5. CI resources and long-term maintenance',expected:'A justified choice with explicit trade-offs and a pinned release plan.',troubleshooting:'If both options fit, select based on the team’s support capacity and prove the riskiest integration with a small prototype.',deliverable:'A concise architecture decision record and a repository/release inventory.'},quiz:{question:'What is BitBake’s primary role?',options:['It is the target device kernel','It executes a metadata-defined task graph','It replaces all upstream source repositories'],answer:1,why:'BitBake orchestrates tasks; recipes and classes describe how software is fetched, built and packaged.'}}),
L({id:'first-build',chapter:'yocto',title:'Your first repeatable Yocto build',description:'Prepare a supported host, initialize the build, choose QEMU and inspect the results.',scene:'build',source:'Yocto-Project-Scarthgap-Learning-Handbook.pdf',goals:['Initialize a build environment','Understand local.conf and bblayers.conf','Build and boot a QEMU image'],steps:[
['Host','Check the host before starting a long build','A Yocto build needs a supported Linux host, required host packages, adequate RAM and substantial fast storage. Exact requirements vary by release and image. Record the release documentation used rather than copying a package list from an unrelated distribution. Avoid network filesystems or compressed paths when the build setup does not support them.','Host compatibility and free disk space are part of build correctness.','df -h .\nfree -h\nuname -srmo','Record available storage, memory and host environment.'],
['Sources','Obtain and record the release baseline','Clone the chosen Poky branch and any compatible layers. Record exact revisions after deciding the baseline. A branch name is useful for following updates, but a release manifest should pin commits. Keep source checkout and build directories conceptually separate so you can inspect metadata without confusing it with generated outputs.','Do not mix Scarthgap examples with newer release-only configuration mechanisms.','git clone --branch scarthgap https://git.yoctoproject.org/poky\ncd poky\ngit rev-parse HEAD','A Scarthgap checkout; record the exact revision you obtained.'],
['Initialize','Source the build environment','oe-init-build-env sets up the shell environment and creates or enters a build directory. The generated conf/local.conf holds build-local choices; conf/bblayers.conf lists participating layers. Running BitBake from an unrelated shell without initialization can fail because the required paths and variables were never established.','Sourcing changes the current shell; running a child script would not preserve those changes.','source oe-init-build-env build-qemu\nls conf','Build configuration files are generated or reused.'],
['Select','Choose a machine and a small image','For a first software-only experiment, use a supported QEMU machine such as qemuarm64. Set MACHINE in local.conf and begin with core-image-minimal. This is a learning image, not a hardened product. Preserve your selected configuration and avoid adding unrelated features until the initial build works.','A small baseline helps you distinguish setup problems from later customization problems.','bitbake-getvar MACHINE\nbitbake core-image-minimal','BitBake parses metadata, schedules tasks and generates image artifacts.'],
['Boot','Boot the result and inspect its identity','runqemu launches the emulator using generated artifacts. A successful image should boot into its intended userspace, where you can inspect the kernel and filesystems. Network and console options vary by machine and host setup. Keep the build log and note the exact image used, so future experiments have a known-good reference.','One successful run becomes a baseline only when its inputs and outputs are identified.','runqemu qemuarm64 core-image-minimal nographic','An emulated target boots using the built image; console behavior depends on configuration.'],
['Repeat','Observe an incremental build','Repeat the same BitBake image command without changing inputs. Many tasks should be skipped or restored rather than rebuilt. Compare this with adding one controlled package later. Learn the difference between downloads, work directories and shared-state cache before attempting to clean them. Deleting caches is expensive and often unrelated to the original problem.','Incremental behavior is evidence that the task graph and cached outputs are being reused.','bitbake core-image-minimal','Review the task summary and which tasks actually needed execution.']
],lab:{objective:'Produce, boot and identify a minimal QEMU image from a recorded Scarthgap checkout.',setup:'Follow the official Scarthgap host prerequisites for your supported distribution. Plan disk and memory headroom.',commands:'git clone --branch scarthgap https://git.yoctoproject.org/poky\ncd poky\ngit rev-parse HEAD\nsource oe-init-build-env build-qemu\n# Set MACHINE = "qemuarm64" in conf/local.conf\nbitbake core-image-minimal\nrunqemu qemuarm64 core-image-minimal nographic',expected:'A bootable emulated target and an inventory of artifacts under tmp/deploy/images/qemuarm64/.',troubleshooting:'For a failure, record the first failing task and inspect its log. Check supported host prerequisites and free storage before altering recipe metadata.',deliverable:'A build manifest, selected configuration, boot log and artifact list.'},quiz:{question:'Why source oe-init-build-env in the current shell?',options:['To flash every attached disk','To establish the build environment used by subsequent commands','To install Linux on the host'],answer:1,why:'The setup must update the environment of the shell where BitBake will run.'}})
];
